s" 1188 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-106502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-2)^{2}=-8(y-5)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/state.json" "file_sha256" "f158fbc8580e7c4a3abd291244f128d5092afa92791560c306e335b2d6ba10b4" "basis" 1188 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14014336/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola\n\n$$\nx+7=-3t,\\,y-2=-9{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "322725844d2a6ae23c36805d73c2a043556c1ef6d4e49bc2f11fc475b2aa754b" "basis" 2292 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+7}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+7}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9} & =-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2) \\\\ {(x+7)}^{2} & =-(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "322725844d2a6ae23c36805d73c2a043556c1ef6d4e49bc2f11fc475b2aa754b" "basis" 2292 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "4da6a88aaf0039840c39c83a1caea296e0c6885740bb22ae8b811407799b1e60" "basis" 2292 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "322725844d2a6ae23c36805d73c2a043556c1ef6d4e49bc2f11fc475b2aa754b" "basis" 2292 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "322725844d2a6ae23c36805d73c2a043556c1ef6d4e49bc2f11fc475b2aa754b" "basis" 2292 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "322725844d2a6ae23c36805d73c2a043556c1ef6d4e49bc2f11fc475b2aa754b" "basis" 2292 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/edb-import/verification.json"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2-y"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/state.json" "file_sha256" "322725844d2a6ae23c36805d73c2a043556c1ef6d4e49bc2f11fc475b2aa754b" "basis" 2292 "verification" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14068270/edb-import/verification.json"}] "sources" [{"question" "q-84462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-1)^{2}=-5(x-1)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-84462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(1,1).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-1=p{t}^{2},\\,y-1=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p(x-1) & =-5(x-1) \\\\ 4p & =-5 \\\\ p & =-\\frac{5}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-1=(-\\frac{5}{4}){t}^{2},\\,y-1=2(-\\frac{5}{4})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-84462" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "86f9117e3bb4b4d8652396fad58252f8aa0c75c0129ea951bf7bca367248b215"} {"question" "q-84462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-84462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+1=\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y+1=\\frac{5}{2}t"] ["math" "x-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t,\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2}"] ["math" "x-1=-\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{4}t"] ["math" "x-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t"] ["math" "x-1=\\frac{5}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=\\frac{5}{4}t"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-84462" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-1=-\\frac{5}{4}{t}^{2},\\,y-1=-\\frac{5}{2}t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-84850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-2=3t,\\,y+2=\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-84850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =\\frac{x-2}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(\\frac{x-2}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{9} & =\\frac{2(y+2)}{3} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =9\\cdot (\\frac{2(y+2)}{3}) \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =6(y+2).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-84850" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "86f9117e3bb4b4d8652396fad58252f8aa0c75c0129ea951bf7bca367248b215"} {"question" "q-84850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-84850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x+2)^{2}=6(y-2)"] ["math" "(x+2)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(y-2)"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}=3(y+2)"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}=6(y+2)"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}=\\frac{8}{3}(y+2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-84850" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-2)^{2}=6(y+2)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-106464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Which of the following sets of parametric equations, defined for $t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ),$ generate the horizontal parabola given by the Cartesian equation $(y-5)^{2}=2(x-2)?$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-106464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The parametric equations of a horizontal parabola $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h)$ that has its vertex at $(h,k)$ are\n\n$$\nx-h=p{t}^{2},\\,y-k=2pt,\n$$\n\nwhere $p$ is constant.\n\nComparing the given Cartesian equation with the general case $(y-k)^{2}=4p(x-h),$ we see that the parabola has its vertex at $(h,k)=(2,5).$ So we have\n\n$$\nx-2=p{t}^{2},\\,y-5=2pt.\n$$\n\nNow, comparing the right-hand sides of the given Cartesian equation and the general case, then solving for $p,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4p(x-h) & =2(x-2) \\\\ 4p(x-2) & =2(x-2) \\\\ 4p & =2 \\\\ p & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the parametric equations of the parabola are\n\n$$\nx-2=(\\frac{1}{2}){t}^{2},\\,y-5=2(\\frac{1}{2})t,\n$$\n\nwhich simplify to\n\n$$\nx-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,y-5=t.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-106464" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "86f9117e3bb4b4d8652396fad58252f8aa0c75c0129ea951bf7bca367248b215"} {"question" "q-106464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-106464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "x+2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y+5=t"] ["math" "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=2t"] ["math" "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=t"] ["math" "x-5=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-2=t"] ["math" "x-5=t,\\,\\,y-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-106464" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "x-2=\\frac{1}{2}{t}^{2},\\,\\,y-5=t"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-106502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the Cartesian equation of the parabola defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx-2=-4t,\\,y-5=-2{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-106502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x-2}{4},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-5}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x-2}{4})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{y-5}{2}$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x-2)^{2}}{16} & =-\\frac{y-5}{2} \\\\ (x-2)^{2} & =-8(y-5).\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-106502" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "86f9117e3bb4b4d8652396fad58252f8aa0c75c0129ea951bf7bca367248b215"} {"question" "q-106502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-106502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "(x-2)^{2}=-8(y-5)"] ["math" "(x-2)^{2}=8(y-5)"] ["math" "(x-5)^{2}=-8(y-2)"] ["math" "(y-5)^{2}=-8(x-2)"] ["math" "(y-5)^{2}=8(x-2)"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-106502" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "(x-2)^{2}=-8(y-5)"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following parametric equation of the parabola\n\n$$\nx+7=-3t,\\,y-2=-9{t}^{2},\\,t\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of this parabola is\n\n$(x\\,+$\n{{field-1}}$)^{2}=$\n{{field-2}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "First, let's make $t$ the subject of the parametric equations. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}t & =-\\frac{x+7}{3},\\,{t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSquaring both sides of the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{t}^{2} & ={(-\\frac{x+7}{3})}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe can now eliminate the parameter $t$ by substituting the above into the equation ${t}^{2}=-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2)$ and rearranging to find the Cartesian equation of the parabola, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{(x+7)^{2}}{9} & =-\\frac{1}{9}(y-2) \\\\ {(x+7)}^{2} & =-(y-2)\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "86f9117e3bb4b4d8652396fad58252f8aa0c75c0129ea951bf7bca367248b215"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "7"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14070454/state.json" "file_sha256" "9f92f1f671fc636caf08df258d0974ff5d4bed9dc6a1174d3250d339d2023545"} {"question" "q-323209" "field" "fie